description:
Write the state machine of lesson 2.05: one connection served with overlapped I/O, reacting to completions one at a time. Requests are framed with a 4-byte little-endian length; the reply to each request is the request's bytes reversed, framed the same way.

```cpp
enum class Op { Read, Write, Close };
struct Request { Op op; std::size_t size; std::vector<std::uint8_t> data; };

class Connection
{
public:
    explicit Connection(std::uint32_t maxMessage);
    Request start();
    Request onRead(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& bytes);
    Request onWritten(std::size_t bytes);
    int served() const;
};
```

Each call returns the **next operation to start**:
- `Read` with `size`: read up to `size` bytes; the result comes back through `onRead` (it may be fewer bytes).
- `Write` with `data`: write these bytes; `onWritten` reports how many were written (it may be fewer; then write the rest).
- `Close`: the connection is finished.

The flow: **start** asks to read the 4-byte header. While the header is incomplete, keep asking for the missing bytes. A length above `maxMessage` means `Close`. Then read the body (asking for exactly the bytes still missing). With the whole body, write the framed reply; when all of it is written, count one served request and ask for the next header. An `onRead` with **0 bytes** means the client disconnected: `Close`.

